# mc-semantic-search — Mode A crontab: server self-update (Story 5.1 / 10.5)
# ============================================================
#
# INSTALL (as root on the server):
#     crontab -e        # paste the block below into your crontab
#   ... or non-interactively:
#     crontab /opt/mc/crontab.example
#
# PATHS: MC_HOME is the repo checkout on the server (docker-compose.yml
# lives there). Every job line uses $MC_HOME — edit the ONE env line below
# if the checkout sits elsewhere (cron passes these assignments into the
# job's environment, so the shell expands them).
#
# NOTIFY HOOK: NOTIFY_WEBHOOK is a Telegram-compatible webhook URL, e.g.
#     NOTIFY_WEBHOOK=https://api.telegram.org/bot<token>/sendMessage
# Every job ends with `|| (…curl……)`: if the job exits non-zero curl fires a
# POST to the webhook. Cron does not read your shell environment: define the
# variable HERE, before the job lines. Set it to a plain value (cron takes
# everything after `=` literally, no quote stripping).
#
# FRESHNESS GATE: the update job ends with `mc health --max-age-hours 72`
# (Story 10.5). It asks the state DB when the last ingest actually succeeded,
# so a run that "completed" without ingesting anything still alerts. Do NOT
# go back to a `find -mmin` gate on the log: `>>` refreshes the log's mtime
# on every run, so that gate can never fire.
#
# PRIVACY: the webhook URL embeds a bot token, so jobs never put it in
# curl's argv (visible to every local user via `ps`). Instead they write
# it into a root-only curl config file (umask 077) and pass that via
# --config. NOTE: cron eats a bare `%`, so the printf format escapes it
# as `\%` below.
#
MC_HOME=/opt/mc
NOTIFY_WEBHOOK=https://api.telegram.org/botCHANGE-ME/sendMessage

# --- jobs --------------------------------------------------------------------
# 1) DAILY UPDATE 06:15 — discover -> download -> chunk -> embed -> upsert,
#    then the freshness gate. compose has no `ingest` service: `docker compose
#    run --rm api ...` maps onto the existing api service + `mc.cli update`
#    (the image ships src/mc/cli.py + src/mc/ingest/). -T: cron has no TTY, so
#    compose must not try to allocate one. `cd` into $MC_HOME so compose reads
#    the repo's docker-compose.yml and bind mounts ./data ./state.
#    YOUTUBE_API_KEY (and any other operator secret) lives in $MC_HOME/.env —
#    the API discovery path never uses cookies (NFR-5).
15 6 * * * flock -n /run/lock/mc-update.lock sh -c 'cd "$MC_HOME" && docker compose run --rm -T api sh -c "python -m mc.cli update && python -m mc.cli health --max-age-hours 72" >> /var/log/mc-update.log 2>&1' || (umask 077 && printf 'url = "\%s"\n' "$NOTIFY_WEBHOOK" > /run/lock/mc-notify.curl && curl -s --config /run/lock/mc-notify.curl -X POST -H 'Content-Type: application/json' -d '{"text":"mc update FAILED (non-zero exit, or no successful ingest within 72h)"}'; rm -f /run/lock/mc-notify.curl)

# 2) NIGHTLY SNAPSHOT 03:00 — Story 5.3 backup entry (rotation keeps last 7).
#    Backup is a pure HTTP call to qdrant, so it runs from the host venv —
#    no image rebuild needed. Snapshot files land in $MC_HOME/snapshots
#    (docker volume ./snapshots:/qdrant/snapshots).
0 3 * * * flock -n /run/lock/mc-snapshot.lock sh -c 'cd "$MC_HOME" && .venv/bin/python -m mc.backup create --keep 7 >> /var/log/mc-snapshot.log 2>&1' || (umask 077 && printf 'url = "\%s"\n' "$NOTIFY_WEBHOOK" > /run/lock/mc-notify.curl && curl -s --config /run/lock/mc-notify.curl -X POST -d '{"text":"mc snapshot FAILED"}'; rm -f /run/lock/mc-notify.curl)

# 3) WEEKLY APPLY SANITY 05:00 Sunday — the Mode B queue should be empty on
#    Mode A, so `mc apply` must be a no-op that exits 0; any other outcome
#    (non-zero exit) is drift and fires the notify hook.
0 5 * * 0 flock -n /run/lock/mc-apply.lock sh -c 'cd "$MC_HOME" && docker compose run --rm -T api python -m mc.cli apply >> /var/log/mc-apply.log 2>&1' || (umask 077 && printf 'url = "\%s"\n' "$NOTIFY_WEBHOOK" > /run/lock/mc-notify.curl && curl -s --config /run/lock/mc-notify.curl -X POST -d '{"text":"mc apply sanity FAILED"}'; rm -f /run/lock/mc-notify.curl)

# NOTES
# - flock -n: a daily run that is still busy (or a cron/snapshot overlap)
#   is skipped instead of stacking duplicate runs against qdrant. Locks
#   live in /run/lock (root-only), NOT world-writable /tmp — there any
#   local user could pre-hold the locks and silently skip the daily jobs.
# - All log paths are absolute (cron may run from / and `>>` relative
#   paths would land somewhere else).
# - First run after a discovery fix ingests the whole backlog (one metadata
#   fetch + one subtitle download per video, minutes to hours): run it by
#   hand once with `docker compose run --rm api python -m mc.cli update`
#   instead of waiting for cron. See docs/tasks/deploy-runbook.md §3.
# - Restore procedure: docs/tasks/backup-restore.md (Story 5.3 runbook).
